  "limitations": [
    "The study used small-scale samples (0.4m x 0.4m) which may not represent full-scale walls (p. 3, p. 8).",
    "The study focused only on the role of vision compared to touch, not other senses like auditory or olfactory (p. 8).",
    "The study did not evaluate architects' real-life material selection process for a project, which might involve different sensory considerations (p. 8)."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated how architecture students assess building materials using vision and touch. It found that while tactile assessment differed significantly from visual assessment for several material attributes (e.g., pattern, gloss, massiveness, warmth), vision generally dominated the overall assessment. Students struggled to correctly identify materials by touch alone and often associated cognitive meanings (e.g., 'cold' concrete) over actual tactile sensations when vision was available. The study highlights that architectural education, beyond the first year, did not significantly alter students' multisensory assessment biases, suggesting a need for more explicit consideration of non-visual material aspects in design education. Limitations include the use of small-scale samples and the focus on only two senses.",
  "stimulus_description": {
